1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,082
Singapore's First Election, 1948:

2
00:00:04,162 --> 00:00:08,450
22,000 Voters in a City of 940,000

3
00:00:08,450 --> 00:00:10,806
On the 20th of March 1948

4
00:00:10,886 --> 00:00:14,200
Singapore held its first election.

5
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,657
Six seats on a Legislative
Council of twenty-two

6
00:00:18,737 --> 00:00:22,910
were open to the vote,
and 22,334 people were

7
00:00:22,990 --> 00:00:28,000
registered to cast one, in
a colony of about 940,000.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,235
The Sunday Times the next morning reported

9
00:00:31,315 --> 00:00:35,025
that 63 per cent of them
had gone to the polls.

10
00:00:35,025 --> 00:00:39,275
Eleven years later, at the 1959 election,

11
00:00:39,355 --> 00:00:45,375
586,098 people were registered
and voting was compulsory.

12
00:00:45,375 --> 00:00:50,846
After the Japanese occupation the
Straits Settlements were broken up,

13
00:00:50,926 --> 00:00:55,350
and in 1946 Singapore became
a Crown colony of its own.

14
00:00:55,350 --> 00:00:59,525
Its new Legislative
Council had 22 members.

15
00:00:59,525 --> 00:01:04,587
Thirteen were officials, three were
nominated by the chambers of commerce,

16
00:01:04,667 --> 00:01:07,714
and six were to be
elected, two each from two

17
00:01:07,794 --> 00:01:11,825
municipal constituencies and
one each from two rural ones.

18
00:01:11,825 --> 00:01:15,160
On the morning of the vote
the Straits Times described

19
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:18,512
it as "the Colony's first
election of representatives

20
00:01:18,592 --> 00:01:21,231
to the Government and
the first step in the

21
00:01:21,311 --> 00:01:24,600
promised progress towards
ultimate self-government."

22
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:28,946
The vote was open to British
subjects aged 21 or over who had

23
00:01:29,026 --> 00:01:33,525
lived in Singapore for at least
a year and who had registered.

24
00:01:33,525 --> 00:01:35,539
Registration was voluntary,

25
00:01:35,619 --> 00:01:39,575
and many people who were
eligible did not register.

26
00:01:39,575 --> 00:01:43,529
The same front page carried
a warning from Thio Chan Bee,

27
00:01:43,609 --> 00:01:46,572
a member of the Singapore
Advisory Council,

28
00:01:46,652 --> 00:01:50,465
that about 100,000 Straits-born
Chinese, who as British

29
00:01:50,545 --> 00:01:54,225
subjects were entitled to
vote, had "missed chance."

30
00:01:54,225 --> 00:02:00,954
He said that only about 5,300 of
the Chinese eligible had registered,

31
00:02:01,034 --> 00:02:04,407
compared with about 10,000 Indians,

32
00:02:04,487 --> 00:02:09,125
3,100 Malays and 3,700
Europeans and Eurasians.

33
00:02:09,125 --> 00:02:13,157
Many other residents, including
most of those born in China,

34
00:02:13,237 --> 00:02:16,800
were not British subjects
and could not vote at all.

35
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:21,970
The Malayan Democratic Union and
several other left-wing organisations

36
00:02:22,050 --> 00:02:26,695
campaigned against the election,
arguing that six elected seats

37
00:02:26,775 --> 00:02:31,500
on a council of 22 fell far short
of representative government.

38
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:34,682
Candidates for the
Progressive Party replied

39
00:02:34,762 --> 00:02:37,875
that the election was a step towards more.

40
00:02:37,875 --> 00:02:41,484
On the eve of the vote, the
Supervisor of Elections,

41
00:02:41,564 --> 00:02:44,677
G. Hawkins, broadcast
an appeal to registered

42
00:02:44,757 --> 00:02:47,950
voters in English, Tamil,
Mandarin and Malay.

43
00:02:47,950 --> 00:02:50,525
Fifteen candidates stood.

44
00:02:50,525 --> 00:02:54,706
The Sunday Times of the 21st of March

45
00:02:54,786 --> 00:03:00,003
reported that of the
22,334 registered voters,

46
00:03:00,083 --> 00:03:04,955
14,126 had cast their
votes, 62.3 per cent,

47
00:03:05,035 --> 00:03:08,950
and 1,321 papers had been spoiled.

48
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:13,837
The six elected members were
Sardon bin Haji Zubir, S. C. Goho,

49
00:03:13,917 --> 00:03:18,175
N. A. Mallal, C. C. Tan, M.
J. Namazie and J. Laycock.

50
00:03:18,175 --> 00:03:22,775
Three belonged to the Progressive
Party and three were independents,

51
00:03:22,855 --> 00:03:24,575
and all six were lawyers.

52
00:03:24,575 --> 00:03:28,132
C. C. Tan, the Progressive
Party's president,

53
00:03:28,212 --> 00:03:32,738
topped the poll with 4,125
votes in Municipal South-West,

54
00:03:32,818 --> 00:03:37,425
and the paper headlined its
report "Boycotters defeated."

55
00:03:37,425 --> 00:03:41,050
The electorate stayed
small for several years.

56
00:03:41,050 --> 00:03:45,664
At the next election, on
the 10th of April 1951,

57
00:03:45,744 --> 00:03:49,087
nine of the council's 25 seats were

58
00:03:49,167 --> 00:03:53,275
elected and 48,155 people were registered.

59
00:03:53,275 --> 00:03:58,428
The large change came in 1955,
under the Rendel constitution,

60
00:03:58,508 --> 00:04:02,202
when 25 of the 32 seats
in a new Legislative

61
00:04:02,282 --> 00:04:07,091
Assembly were elected and the
number of registered voters

62
00:04:07,171 --> 00:04:11,975
rose above 300,000, more than
six times the 1951 figure.

63
00:04:11,975 --> 00:04:17,000
That election made David Marshall
Singapore's first Chief Minister.

64
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,964
At the 1959 election, the first
under full internal self-government,

65
00:04:23,044 --> 00:04:28,120
all 51 seats were elected,
586,098 people were registered,

66
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:32,200
and voting was compulsory
for the first time.

67
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:36,970
Where it fits in the bigger story:
Singapore's political history

68
00:04:37,050 --> 00:04:41,825
is usually told from 1959, when
the whole Assembly was elected.

69
00:04:41,825 --> 00:04:46,279
The first election, eleven years
earlier, was held for a handful of

70
00:04:46,359 --> 00:04:50,881
seats by a few thousand British
subjects who had chosen to register,

71
00:04:50,961 --> 00:04:55,225
while most of the colony's
residents could not vote or did not.

72
00:04:55,225 --> 00:05:00,476
The growth from 22,334
voters in 1948 to 586,098

73
00:05:00,556 --> 00:05:06,473
in 1959 is the part of the
story that is easy to miss,

74
00:05:06,553 --> 00:05:11,550
and it happened in little
more than a decade.
